@RULE Swirl

Swirl, the Generations rule 23/34/8: a live cell (state 1) with two or three live neighbours
stays live, and one with any other count starts to die; a dying cell (states 2 to 7) moves one
state on each generation and back to 0 after 7; a dead cell with three or four live neighbours
is born. Only live cells are counted. Eight states make 8^9 neighbourhoods, too many to expand
into a table, which is what a tree is for.

